lessons.deep:
A **sanctioned** AI tool or model has passed the organization's review: security and privacy assessment, legal and contract review, and risk rating. It is typically provided under an **enterprise agreement** with commitments such as no training on customer data, defined retention, data residency, and admin controls like **SSO**, role-based access, audit logging and DLP integration.

An **unsanctioned** tool has not been approved; using it for company work is shadow AI. Some organizations add a middle tier, **tolerated** or **restricted** tools allowed only for public, non-sensitive data.

The corporate AI policy should state:
- the **approved tools list** and the use cases each is approved for;
- which **data classification levels** may be used with each tool;
- prohibited uses (e.g. making hiring decisions without human review);
- how to request approval for a new tool;
- consequences of non-compliance.

Enforcement uses technical controls: **CASB/SSE** app control to allow sanctioned tools and block or warn on unsanctioned ones, tenant restrictions so only corporate accounts work, DLP on prompts, and browser-extension management. Policies only work alongside training and a usable sanctioned option.

